# Reader docs

These are the pages a stranger reads before the source. `README.md` is the crates.io page. `README.ja.md` is its Japanese pair. `docs/comparison.md` and `docs/comparison.ja.md` hold the library comparison that used to sit in the README.

`missing_docs` passing is not this check. An agent reads the pages against the items below before `cargo package` and `cargo publish --dry-run`. The procedure is `.agents/skills/publish-review/SKILL.md`.

## README shape

Order:

1. What the crate does
2. A `Cargo.toml` dependency block for the version being published
3. One example a reader can paste after adding that dependency
4. What a caller needs in order to make that call
5. One link to the comparison doc
6. License

English and Japanese use the same sections. The comparison's tables, figures, and reproduce steps live in the comparison doc, not in the README.

Before the first example, state the points a caller otherwise gets wrong: `X` is column-major, `fit` consumes the trainer, observation noise lives in `GaussianLikelihood`, a 0.x minor may break the public API, and `internals` is outside that contract.

The example uses `?`, not `unwrap`. It does not depend on a checkout of this repository.

## Public API usage

The public contract is every item an external crate can name with default features. `internals` is outside it.

Document every item in that set in the Usage section of `README.md` and of `README.ja.md`. Give every item a usage example in both files. A usage example is a `rust` code block that constructs the item, calls it, matches it, or implements it. A sentence that only names the item is not an example. One block may cover several items when each of those items appears in the block. English and Japanese use the same blocks.

When the families differ, say which type the method belongs to. When the item has a default that changes the result, include that default.

Rustdoc on each item follows `.cursor/rules/rust-docs.mdc`: the item is documented, and it has a usage example. The keys and tensors of a saved directory stay in the persist-format doc. The README links there.

A public item with no README entry, no usage example in the README, or no rustdoc example fails the publish review.

## Links that survive packaging

`docs/` is not in the published package. The README has no relative link to a file outside the package, and no image. Figures stay relative inside the comparison doc.

The README's link to the comparison doc is one absolute GitHub URL, the same form as the links to `docs/design.md`. License links target `LICENSE-MIT` and `LICENSE-APACHE`, which are in the package.

## Stranger context

A reader of these pages does not need an issue number, a pull request number, a phase ID, a roadmap ID, `roadmap.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursor/rules`, or Grill.

Developer `just` recipes stay out of the README. The comparison doc may keep the `just` commands that regenerate its figures. It does not keep issue numbers or phase IDs.

`docs/design.md`, `docs/roadmap.md`, and `docs/adr/` are the decision record. They keep their IDs.

User commands such as `cargo run --example fit_predict` stay.

## Accuracy

Names, defaults, layout, errors, the MSRV, and the version match the code and `Cargo.toml`. A command in the page is a real command. A number in the comparison doc matches a committed measurement, or the sentence names where it came from.

The English README, the Japanese README, the crate-level rustdoc, and `Cargo.toml` `description` do not contradict each other. `description` is one sentence, the same claim as the README opening, with no internal workflow words.

## Japanese

Read `README.ja.md` and `docs/comparison.ja.md` with `.agents/skills/natural-japanese/SKILL.md`. Do not leave them as a gloss of the English.
